{"arm": "X2-P", "compounds": 0, "compounds_kept": 0, "diagnoses": [{"cell": 15, "cost_per_unit": "About 0.85 s cumulative / 51,200, or about 17 us per statement under the profiler. That is roughly ten Python function calls per statement: skip_chars x3-4, parse_key, parse_key_part, parse_value, plus Flags and nest lookups. Each numeric value also runs up to three regex matches (datetime, localtim", "deal_round": 2, "doubling": "Linear: doubling the document doubles the statements, values and characters, so wall time doubles. No superlinear term appears, since flag lookups depend on key depth, not document size.", "gap": "Most of the time goes to Python-level dispatch per character and per token rather than to the unavoidable object construction. Examples: failing datetime and localtime regex attempts before every number, per-character string scanning, and repeated helper calls. The gap is several-fold, spread thinly", "least_work": "One linear pass over the source characters, plus building each output object once. Runs of ordinary characters (key names, string bodies, digits) can be consumed by single C-level scans rather than one Python step per character.", "library": "tomli", "run": 1, "unit_count": "key_value_rule: 51,200 calls (6,400 per parse x 8). parse_value: 108,800 (13,600 per parse). skip_chars: 502,400. parse_key_part: 70,400. parse_basic_str: 19,200.", "unit_of_work": "One TOML statement, usually a 'key = value' line, parsed by key_value_rule. Each statement goes through parse_key_value_pair, then parse_key and parse_key_part, then parse_value. Array items and inline-table entries add further parse_value calls."}], "experiment": "ideas-x2-round", "kept": 0, "lane_views": [{"lane": "obvious", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "view_sha": "62029649"}, {"lane": "assume_less", "served_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "view_sha": "62029649"}, {"lane": "leap", "served_model": "claude-opus-5-5", "view_sha": "62029649"}, {"lane": "far", "served_model": "claude-so